   {
    "question": "The function $f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined by $f(x) = 5 - \\frac{2x}{3}$.\nDecide whether $f$ is injective, surjective and bijective, giving a reason for each.",
    "level": "higher",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Test injectivity: assume equal outputs",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$f$ is injective if $f(a) = f(b)$ forces $a = b$."
        },
        {
         "tex": "5 - \\frac{2a}{3} &= 5 - \\frac{2b}{3}",
         "sym": "Eq(5 - 2*a/3, 5 - 2*b/3)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "-\\frac{2a}{3} &= -\\frac{2b}{3}",
         "sym": "Eq(-2*a/3, -2*b/3)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "a &= b",
         "sym": "Eq(a, b)"
        },
        {
         "text": "Equal outputs force equal inputs, so $f$ is injective."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Test surjectivity: solve f(x) = y",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$f$ is surjective if every $y$ in the codomain $\\mathbb{R}$ equals $f(x)$ for some $x$ in the domain."
        },
        {
         "tex": "y &= 5 - \\frac{2x}{3}",
         "sym": "Eq(y, 5 - 2*x/3)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "\\frac{2x}{3} &= 5 - y",
         "sym": "Eq(2*x/3, 5 - y)"
        },
        {
         "tex": "x &= \\frac{15 - 3y}{2}",
         "sym": "Eq(x, (15 - 3*y)/2)"
        },
        {
         "text": "This $x$ is a real number for every real $y$, so every $y$ is reached and $f$ is surjective."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Classify",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$f$ is both injective and surjective, so it is bijective."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "f \\text{ is injective, surjective and bijective}"
    },
    "finalSym": "1",
    "answerSym": "1 // len(solve(Eq(5 - 2*x/3, 7), x))"
   },
   {
    "question": "The function $g : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is defined by $g(x) = x^2 - 6x + 5$.\nDecide whether $g$ is injective, surjective and bijective, giving a reason for each.",
    "level": "higher",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
     "steps": [
      {
       "title": "Test injectivity: look for two inputs with the same output",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "g(0) &= 0^2 - 6(0) + 5",
         "sym": "0**2 - 6*0 + 5",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "&= 5",
         "sym": "5"
        },
        {
         "tex": "g(6) &= 6^2 - 6(6) + 5",
         "sym": "6**2 - 6*6 + 5",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "&= 5",
         "sym": "5"
        },
        {
         "text": "Two different inputs, $0$ and $6$, give the same output, so $g$ is not injective."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Test surjectivity: find the smallest output",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "x^2 - 6x + 5 &= (x - 3)^2 - 4",
         "sym": "Eq(x**2 - 6*x + 5, (x - 3)**2 - 4)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "text": "Since $(x - 3)^2 \\ge 0$, every output satisfies $g(x) \\ge -4$. Test a value below $-4$, such as $-5$."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Try to solve g(x) = -5",
       "lines": [
        {
         "tex": "(x - 3)^2 - 4 &= -5",
         "sym": "Eq((x - 3)**2 - 4, -5)",
         "new": true
        },
        {
         "tex": "(x - 3)^2 &= -1",
         "sym": "Eq((x - 3)**2, -1)"
        },
        {
         "text": "A square cannot be negative, so there is no real $x$ with $g(x) = -5$. Then $-5$ is in the codomain but is never reached, so $g$ is not surjective."
        }
       ]
      },
      {
       "title": "Classify",
       "lines": [
        {
         "text": "$g$ is neither injective nor surjective, so it is not bijective."
        }
       ]
      }
     ],
     "finalAnswer": "g \\text{ is neither injective nor surjective, so not bijective}"
    },
    "finalSym": "0",
    "answerSym": "1 // len(solve(Eq(x**2 - 6*x + 5, 5), x))"
   },
   {
    "question": "The function $h : \\{1, 2, 3\\} \\to \\{1, 2, 3, 4\\}$ is defined by $h(x) = x + 1$.\nDecide whether $h$ is injective, surjective and bijective, giving a reason for each.",
    "level": "higher",
    "marks": 10,
    "workedSolution": {
